      m_Calls: []
  m_text: Blood low in oxygen and high in carbon dioxide returns to the right atrium
    of the heart. It then moves to the right ventricle, which pumps the deoxygenated
    blood into the pulmonary arteries. These arteries carry the blood to the lungs
    where carbon dioxide is exchanged for oxygen. Oxygenated blood then returns to
    the left atrium via the pulmonary veins and from there to the left ventricle,
    preparing it for the systemic circuit which delivers oxygenated blood to the
    rest of the body.
